# Loaded when NODE_ENV=test — which every test script sets. Copy to
# backend/.env.test. Test suites NEVER read the development backend/.env, so
# a mispointed test can never touch a developer's working data through that
# file; each suite still creates its own throwaway database/schema on top of
# these connection coordinates.

# REQUIRED. Same rules as development: no default, ≥ 32 characters.
JWT_SECRET=

# Cluster connection coordinates for the suites. Point these at the same
# local instance you develop against; the suites create and drop their own
# databases and schemas on it.
DB_HOST=localhost
DB_PORT=5432
DB_USER=app
DB_PASSWORD=app
DB_NAME=app_dev

# PGRST_JWT_SECRET must equal JWT_SECRET. There is no PGRST_DB_URI: the RLS
# fixture builds its own URI against its own throwaway database and its own
# password, and the orchestrators compose theirs. See backend/.env.example.
PGRST_JWT_SECRET=

# The thin app's listen port. The Angular SSR server reads SSR_PORT, not this
# key — one key, one listener.
PORT=3000
SSR_PORT=4000

# LOGIN password of the pgrst_authenticator role. Required, no default: the RLS
# proof changes this on the CLUSTER while it runs and restores the value from
# here when it finishes, so this key is what the suite leaves behind.
AUTHENTICATOR_PASSWORD=
POSTGREST_URL=http://127.0.0.1:3001

# The application's own public origin. REQUIRED in every mode, no default: every
# email template carries an absolute link, and the previous tree's
# http://localhost:4200 default put a link to the developer's own machine inside
# every message the application ever sent. Must be https in production.
APP_BASE_URL=http://localhost:4000

# Transactional email (src/email). There is ONE transport — nodemailer
# smtpTransport — in every mode; only the destination changes, and it comes from
# configuration alone. In development and test that destination is the local
# SMTP sink: an in-process, loopback-only listener `npm run dev` starts
# (backend/scripts/smtp-sink.ts, imported by scripts/dev.mjs). It stores what it
# accepts and never relays, which is why production may not name a loopback
# EMAIL_HOST — SMTP's 250 is an acknowledgement, not a delivery.
#
# EMAIL_HOST and EMAIL_PORT are REQUIRED in every mode with no default. The sink
# does not authenticate, so EMAIL_USER/EMAIL_PASSWORD are unset here; in
# production they are required with no default (a relay credential is not
# something to invent).
# EMAIL_SECURE defaults to true on port 465 and false elsewhere (STARTTLS).
# EMAIL_FROM defaults to no-reply@<hostname of APP_BASE_URL> outside production.
# APP_NAME defaults to that same host; set it to the name users know.
# EMAIL_TLS_REJECT_UNAUTHORIZED defaults to true and is REFUSED as false in
# production; it exists for the sink's self-signed certificate.
EMAIL_HOST=127.0.0.1
EMAIL_PORT=1025

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Circuit breaker and purge sweeper
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# All seven values below are REQUIRED with no defaults. A missing key aborts the
# boot rather than substituting a default, because the defaults are the failure
# mode that matters: a breaker configured from nothing would compare an
# unconfigured threshold against real usage and produce a verdict with no owner.
#
# The thresholds are PERCENTAGES of the memory cgroup limit and are validated at
# boot: green < yellow < red, each 1-100. `90/60/60` aborts the boot rather than
# shipping a breaker whose red zone is never the highest value.
#
# The denominator is the cgroup limit (v2 `memory.max`, else v1
# `memory.limit_in_bytes`), falling back to `os.totalmem()`. On a host with no
# cgroup memory limit set, the v1 file reads 9223372036854771712 — the unlimited
# sentinel — and treating it as a limit yields a usage of ~0.00000003%, so the
# breaker can never reach any threshold. That sentinel is detected and rejected.
#
# DISK is measured and reported but is never a trip input: the app cleans up its
# own logs and temp files, and true external disk pressure is not the app's
# problem to shed load for.
#
# BREAKER_PROTECTED is a WHITELIST and the default is STOP. There is deliberately
# no stoppable list — the protected set is short and stable, whereas a stoppable
# list grows with every new async service and each omission would be silently
# unsheddable. An unknown token in this list aborts the boot.
BREAKER_MEMORY_GREEN=60
BREAKER_MEMORY_YELLOW=80
BREAKER_MEMORY_RED=90
BREAKER_POLL_SECONDS=30
BREAKER_COOLDOWN_SECONDS=120
BREAKER_PROBE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=60
BREAKER_PROTECTED=auth.login,auth.refresh,auth.logout,auth.me,breaker.meter,breaker.gate

# The purge sweeper reads privacy_purge_queue and acts under
# `FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED`, so N instances are safe. These three DO have defaults.
# The drain budget is per tick: without it a large backlog runs until the
# statement timeout, and a ticket that is both slow and infinite is a ticket that
# holds its claim forever.
# PURGE_POLL_SECONDS=60
# PURGE_DRAIN_BUDGET=100
# RETENTION_PURGE_MAX_ATTEMPTS=3

# TWO keys, both REQUIRED in every mode, both with NO default, and both are keys
# that decide whether personal data is destroyed. They are the two halves of one
# scan, and they are separate keys because they age two different populations on
# two different clocks:
#
#   RETENTION_TOMBSTONE_DAYS       a soft-deleted user — a TOMBSTONE whose erasure
#                                  is already recorded — older than this window is
#                                  enqueued as reason `inactivity_purge`, claimed
#                                  by `authz.claim_due_purge()` and deleted for
#                                  good. FLOOR 1825 days (five years).
#
#   RETENTION_INACTIVE_ACCOUNT_DAYS a LIVE account whose last OBSERVED activity is
#                                  older than this window, and which was never
#                                  soft-deleted, is enqueued as reason
#                                  `inactive_account_purge`. FLOOR 1095 days
#                                  (three years).
#
# WHY THE FLOORS ARE ENFORGED RATHER THAN SUGGESTED. Until 0017 the bound on the
# first key was 1..36500, which meant `RETENTION_TOMBSTONE_DAYS=1` was VALID
# CONFIGURATION and hard-destroyed every tombstone in the repository on the next
# sweep. A lower bound of 1 is a bound on syntax, not on harm. Both keys are now
# refused at BOOT, naming the key, the value received and the floor, and refused
# AGAIN inside `authz.retention_due_tombstones()` / `authz.retention_due_accounts()`
# — because boot validation is one process, and the function is reachable by
# anything granted EXECUTE.
#
# WHY NO DEFAULT IS SUPPLIED. A default nobody chose silently applies, and this is
# the shape of that failure an auditor reads: the published retention policy says
# one window and the code deletes on another (GDPR Art. 5(1)(e),
# CPRA §1798.100(a)(3)). The three operational keys — PURGE_POLL_SECONDS,
# PURGE_DRAIN_BUDGET and RETENTION_PURGE_MAX_ATTEMPTS — have defaults because they
# change WHEN a job runs; the two here change WHOSE DATA ENDS, which is a decision
# and not a scheduling fact.
#
# WHAT IS *NOT* PURGED BY EITHER: an account whose `users.last_activity_at` IS NULL.
# NULL means "activity was never observed", and a measurement that was never taken
# cannot show that an account is dormant — so an account created before 0017 and
# never authenticated again is retained indefinitely, by decision. The column is
# never backfilled from `created_at`: that would make every pre-existing account
# look decades dormant and would get real, active accounts destroyed.
#
# Neither key is the erasure path. A privacy deletion REQUEST has no
# implementation in this template; these windows are retention, not erasure.
RETENTION_TOMBSTONE_DAYS=1825
RETENTION_INACTIVE_ACCOUNT_DAYS=1095

# HOW OFTEN THE SCAN RUNS — and this key is on the OTHER side of the line the two
# windows above are on. It changes WHEN the question is asked, not WHOSE DATA ENDS,
# so it HAS a default (3600) and an absent key is not a boot failure. A MALFORMED
# one still is, naming the key and the value: `RETENTION_ENQUEUE_INTERVAL_SECONDS=1h`
# is a retention sweep that never runs, and nothing else in this file would say so.
# Range 1..31536000; a value past a year is far more likely to be a units mistake.
#
# It was a constant in `purge-enqueuer.service.ts` until the scheduler took the
# single-instance lock: changing the cadence is now a configuration decision
# somebody made rather than an edit to a source file.
RETENTION_ENQUEUE_INTERVAL_SECONDS=3600

# HOW OFTEN THE CONSENT RECORDS OF ERASED PEOPLE ARE EXPIRED — the same shape as
# the key above, and a SEPARATE key on purpose: `consent.purge` and
# `purge.enqueuer` are two different jobs, and "how often does the scan run" and
# "how often are the consent records of erased people expired" are two different
# questions. One key would make them one question, and changing one cadence would
# silently change the other's. So it HAS a default (86400) and an absent key is not
# a boot failure. A MALFORMED one still is, naming the key and the value:
# `RETENTION_CONSENT_PURGE_INTERVAL_SECONDS=1h` is a retention sweep that never
# runs, with the window it enforces looking entirely configured. Range 1..31536000.
RETENTION_CONSENT_PURGE_INTERVAL_SECONDS=86400

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rate limiting (src/common/throttle.ts)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# FOUR keys, and every one of them HAS a default. A MALFORMED one is refused at
# BOOT rather than absorbed: the limiter is a control, and a control configured
# from a value nobody could parse is not a stricter limiter — it is a limiter
# that fails open, which is the one direction a limiter may not fail in.
#
# They are BLANK here on purpose. A blank means "the default applies until an
# operator chooses a number", which is the whole point of a namespace whose keys
# may default; a committed number would be a number nobody chose, shipping
# silently to every deployment that copied this file.
#
#   THROTTLE_LIMIT             requests per window, per address, per route. The
#                              global allowance every route inherits. Default 300.
#   THROTTLE_TTL_SECONDS       the window every limit is counted over, in
#                              seconds. Default 60.
#   THROTTLE_CREDENTIAL_LIMIT  the allowance on the routes that check or move a
#                              credential. Default 30.
#   THROTTLE_LINK_LIMIT        the allowance on the routes that send a one-time
#                              link. Default 15.
#
# The counts run 1..100000 and the window 1..3600 seconds. A zero is not "no
# limit" — it is a limiter that limits nothing — and a value past the ceiling is
# far more likely to be a units mistake than a policy.
THROTTLE_LIMIT=
THROTTLE_TTL_SECONDS=
THROTTLE_CREDENTIAL_LIMIT=
THROTTLE_LINK_LIMIT=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Erasure (src/privacy)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THREE keys, all REQUIRED in every mode, all with NO default, blank here for the
# same reason JWT_SECRET is: a committed file must not carry a secret.
#
#   PRIVACY_HMAC_PEPPER       the key every tombstone is derived from:
#                             deleted-<HMAC-SHA256(pepper, users.id)[0..32]>
#                             It signs the OPAQUE user id and never the email
#                             address: an email is drawn from a small,
#                             enumerable space, so hashing one is a dictionary
#                             attack that recovers the very address somebody asked
#                             to be forgotten, while a 122-bit database key has no
#                             such space. At least 32 characters, and NOT the same
#                             value as JWT_SECRET — one key signing both the session
#                             tokens and the tombstone digests is one leak and two
#                             compromises, and bootstrap refuses it.
#
#   PRIVACY_HMAC_PEPPER_KID   WHICH key this is, so a rotated pepper can still be
#                             told apart from the one before it. Change the pepper
#                             and the kid in the same deployment. 3-64 characters
#                             of letters, digits, dot, underscore or hyphen.
#
#   PRIVACY_TOMBSTONE_DOMAIN  where the placeholder address lives, e.g.
#                             'tombstone.example.test'. Appended after '@', so a
#                             value carrying its own '@' is refused at boot.
#
# WHY NO DEFAULT, EVER. A default pepper is a PUBLISHED key: anyone who has read
# this repository could recompute HMAC(pepper, users.id) for any row and match the
# result against the tombstone. A process that cannot name the key refuses to
# start, at MODULE-EVALUATION time in erasure.module.ts.
#
# These must be present in .env.test, because the unit suite boots AppModule and
# erasure.module.ts is evaluated on the way in — a test run that stops with
# "PRIVACY_HMAC_PEPPER is required" has been told exactly which key is missing.
PRIVACY_HMAC_PEPPER=
PRIVACY_HMAC_PEPPER_KID=
PRIVACY_TOMBSTONE_DOMAIN=
AUTH_TOKEN_PEPPER=
AUTH_TOKEN_PEPPER_KID=
